# shared-assets.allow — assets under public/ that are byte-identical to the
# paired private original AND have been looked at and cleared.
#
# WHY THESE ARE LISTED RATHER THAN REPLACED
# All 12 are technical equipment reference photos plus the favicon. None carries
# a wordmark, a logo, a person, a vehicle or a building. They were shared with
# the private original from the start because they are generic product imagery,
# not the client's own photography — which is exactly the distinction the two
# files deleted on 2026-08-07 failed:
#
#   the logo file      the real company's actual logo, with its name rendered
#                      inside the image. DELETED, not allowlisted.
#   a services photo   named the real client in its filename. DELETED.
#
# Both had been left in place as "orphaned (no longer referenced)". An
# unreferenced file still serves at a guessable public URL. Not referenced is
# not removed — so neither was allowlisted, both were deleted.
#
# A hash listed here is exempt FOREVER, so add one only after looking at the file.
# Format:  <sha256>  <reason>
